 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T17:06:13.241275Z",
 "text": "The clause is **not enforceable** as written. Under Ontario's *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006*, a landlord who holds a rent deposit must pay the tenant interest on it at the prescribed rate, usually annually and when the deposit is returned or applied. The landlord's reason for holding the deposit, here applying it to the last month's rent, does not remove that obligation. Section 4 also makes the Act's protections apply despite any agreement to the contrary, so a tenant cannot waive the interest right by signing this clause.\n\nThe landlord may still hold the deposit and apply it to the last month's rent, provided the deposit does not exceed the permitted amount. But the landlord must pay interest on it throughout the tenancy.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 107",
